  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1245 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nMid-level ridging builds back over the Pacific SW into Desert SW \nmid- week and over the area by late week through the early \nweekend. Black Friday features highs once more rising to 60F-70F \nfor most of the lowlands, 50F-60F for the highest elevations. \nWest/southwest downsloping winds gusting to 20 to 30 mph leeward \nof the western higher terrain will help to warm temperatures, \nwhile allowing enough WAA and boundary layer mixing to keep lows \nwithin the 35F-45F range overnight. As ridging intensifies over \nthe Desert SW and heights over the area increase with the warming \nand expanding air column, highs increase back into the 65F-75F \nrange Saturday. Closer to the surface, this will be aided by \ndevelopment of lee troughing over eastern NM into W TX and \nsubsequent stronger westerly downsloping winds 25 to 35 mph or \nhigher for western higher terrain and leeward. At this time, \nmagnitude and duration of winds are not sufficient to warrant high\nwind products (only a 45%-50% chance of winds above 35 mph for \nmore than 3 hours, and less than 20% chance of gusts above 50 \nmph), but we will continue to monitor for possible later issuance.\nStrong winds continue Saturday night, before diminishing below 20\nto 30 mph and shifting to west/northwest with passage of a weak \nPacific cold front within west/northwest flow downstream of \ncontinued ridging over the Desert SW. Lows Saturday night remain \nsimilar to Friday night if not a few degrees warmer, before highs \nSunday decrease back down into the 55F-65F range for much of the \narea under lighter northerly winds. \n\nBy early next week, lee troughing redevelops and ridging over the\narea expands again, allowing highs to rise back into the 65F-75F \nrange, while maintaining lows in a \"chilly\" 45F-55F range. The \ncalendar will soon mark the meteorological beginning of winter \n(December 1), but it will feel more like autumn with no \nprecipitation chances in sight following our early week brush with\nwinter weather.",
